"title":"Perfume"},"Plants":{"file":"dictionary/4577249ac9b7773d00fd8bc627ee1383687474dfdb93b77f98c93d99e4ce0612.json","title":"Plants"},"Shulammite":{"file":"dictionary/8d7fe01564aaddb55ad634b56ac0a301331934d517eca2c7dfcb6eaa3a1ffd5d.json","title":"Shulammite*"},"SongofSolomon":{"file":"dictionary/a384b03154699bde0b5371ddf571bfb3859c6754e22d7b3669cfdd7c673d591f.json","title":"Song of Solomon*"},"Sun":{"file":"dictionary/45e9ac9ea81926efcacdabd0dd1023b90adaca811046df54a76ffc2874cad4c2.json","title":"Sun"},"TirzahPlace":{"file":"dictionary/833ae8bb894a6d41e704b7364b3d852674ea0816b4f053860e1ed4bb97d266e0.json","title":"Tirzah (Place)"}},"dictionaryRelated":[{"id":"Amminadib","title":"Amminadib*"},{"id":"Animals","title":"Animals"},{"id":"BatheBathing","title":"Bathe, Bathing"},{"id":"Beauty","title":"Beauty"},{"id":"Birds","title":"Birds"},{"id":"Moon","title":"Moon"},{"id":"Perfume","title":"Perfume"},{"id":"Plants","title":"Plants"},{"id":"Shulammite","title":"Shulammite*"},{"id":"SongofSolomon","title":"Song of Solomon*"},{"id":"Sun","title":"Sun"},{"id":"TirzahPlace","title":"Tirzah (Place)"}],"dictionaryWords":[{"evidence":[{"matchingRule":"reviewed_exact_headword","phrase":"Jerusalem","senseEvidence":{"basis":"reviewed_unambiguous_name","originalCitations":[],"ruleId":"Jerusalem::Jerusalem::reviewed_unambiguous_name","sourceHeadwordId":"Jerusalem"},"text":"You are as beautiful, my darling, as Tirzah, as lovely as Jerusalem, as majestic as troops with banners.","verse":4}],"id":"Jerusalem","label":"Jerusalem","matchReviews":[{"ruleId":"Jerusalem::Jerusalem::reviewed_unambiguous_name","scope":"reviewed_unambiguous_name","senseReview":"Reviewed complete original name headword and source subject. Article covers the biblical person(s), named place, or named spiritual figure denoted by this exact spelling; multiple persons under one unchanged source article remain together. Actual proper-name capitalization and whole-name boundaries required. Explicit occurrence-level ambiguity exclusions override this rule.","sourceHeadwordId":"Jerusalem","term":"Jerusalem"}],"matchingRule":"reviewed_exact_headword","matchingRules":["reviewed_exact_headword"],"sourceProvenanceId":"Jerusalem","sourceTitle":"Jerusalem"}],"format":"bsb-study-chapter-v2","notes":[{"chapterAssociationStatus":"canonical_reference","content":[{"c":[{"c":[{"href":"?bref=Song.5.2-6.3","r":"Song.5.2-6.3","rawR":"Song.5.2-6.3","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Song.5.2-6.3","value":"Song.5.2-6.3"}],"t":"ref","v":"5:2–6:3"}],"sourceClass":"sn-ref","t":"span"}," One verse earlier, the couple are fully enjoying their intimate union. Now the woman feels the torment of separation. In the dramatic interpretation, the woman has been separated from the man twice before and has searched for him (",{"href":"?bref=Song.1.4-7","r":"Song.1.4-7","rawR":"Song.1.4-7","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Song.1.4-7","value":"Song.1.4-7"}],"t":"ref","v":"1:4-7"},"; ",{"href":"?bref=Song.3.1-4","r":"Song.3.1-4","rawR":"Song.3.1-4","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Song.3.1-4","value":"Song.3.1-4"}],"t":"ref","v":"3:1-4"},"). This last time, the separation takes place in her dreams—it is only a nightmare. Her dream seems to extend to ",{"href":"?bref=Song.6.3","r":"Song.6.3","rawR":"Song.6.3","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Song.6.3","value":"Song.6.3"}],"t":"ref","v":"6:3"},", given the natural flow of questions and answers between the woman and the young women of Jerusalem. • If the Song is an anthology, these poems explore love rather than giving accounts of actual events. This poem reminds us that an intimate relationship is not always easy or perfectly satisfying."],"sourceClass":"sn-text","t":"p"}],"id":"StudyNote:Song.5.2-6.3","refs":[{"book":"SNG","endChapter":6,"endVerse":3,"startChapter":5,"startVerse":2}],"sourceAttributes":{"name":"Song.5.2-6.3","product":"TyndaleOpenStudyNotes","typename":"StudyNote"},"sourceBookAppCode":"SNG","sourceChapterCoverage":[5,6],"sourceItemFragmentSha256":"6b0720b1ed40abb591cad549f6de57e69b45b9e53c7426789ef21b06edd475fc","sourceItemName":"Song.5.2-6.3","sourceMember":"Tyndale Open Study Notes/StudyNotes.xml","sourceOrder":8155,"sourceReferenceException":false,"sourceReferenceMappingEvidence":{"appCode":"SNG","canonicalOSIS":"Song","method":"official_book_intro_ref_title","sourceOSIS":"Song"},"sourceReferenceStatus":"canonical","sourceRefsExact":"Song.5.2-6.3","sourceRelease":"1.25","sourceTitleExact":null,"supplementPointersNotIncludedAsContent":[],"title":"Song.5.2-6.3","type":"StudyNote"},{"chapterAssociationStatus":"canonical_reference","content":[{"c":[{"c":[{"href":"?bref=Song.6.1-3","r":"Song.6.1-3","rawR":"Song.6.1-3","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Song.6.1-3","value":"Song.6.1-3"}],"t":"ref","v":"6:1-3"}],"sourceClass":"sn-ref","t":"span"}," The conversation that ends this poem indicates that it is not an actual event. The dream ends as the young women of Jerusalem ask the woman where her man has gone. The woman tells them not to concern themselves with finding him—he is back in bed with her, enjoying his ",{"c":["garden"],"sourceClass":"sn-excerpt","t":"span"}," with its ",{"c":["spice beds"],"sourceClass":"sn-excerpt","t":"span"}," and ",{"c":["lilies"],"sourceClass":"sn-excerpt","t":"span"},{"c":[","],"sourceClass":"ital","t":"span"}," a description of sexual intimacy."],"sourceClass":"sn-text","t":"p"}],"id":"StudyNote:Song.6.1-3","refs":[{"book":"SNG","endChapter":6,"endVerse":3,"startChapter":6,"startVerse":1}],"sourceAttributes":{"name":"Song.6.1-3","product":"TyndaleOpenStudyNotes","typename":"StudyNote"},"sourceBookAppCode":"SNG","sourceChapterCoverage":[6],"sourceItemFragmentSha256":"048038ed9f18d9cac34ac2cf227a8fa473693296d6eeaa751eb6e5826c8c3033","sourceItemName":"Song.6.1-3","sourceMember":"Tyndale Open Study Notes/StudyNotes.xml","sourceOrder":8168,"sourceReferenceException":false,"sourceReferenceMappingEvidence":{"appCode":"SNG","canonicalOSIS":"Song","method":"official_book_intro_ref_title","sourceOSIS":"Song"},"sourceReferenceStatus":"canonical","sourceRefsExact":"Song.6.1-3","sourceRelease":"1.25","sourceTitleExact":null,"supplementPointersNotIncludedAsContent":[],"title":"Song.6.1-3","type":"StudyNote"},{"chapterAssociationStatus":"canonical_reference","content":[{"c":[{"c":[{"href":"?bref=Song.6.4-10","r":"Song.6.4-10","rawR":"Song.6.4-10","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Song.6.4-10","value":"Song.6.4-10"}],"t":"ref","v":"6:4-10"}],"sourceClass":"sn-ref","t":"span"}," The man again describes the physical beauty of the woman. He repeats parts of the description from ch ",{"href":"?bref=Song.4.1-16","r":"Song.4.1-16","rawR":"Song.4.1-16","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Song.4.1-16","value":"Song.4.1-16"}],"t":"ref","v":"4"}," almost verbatim, showing the same high regard for his wife."],"sourceClass":"sn-text","t":"p"}],"id":"StudyNote:Song.6.4-10","refs":[{"book":"SNG","endChapter":6,"endVerse":10,"startChapter":6,"startVerse":4}],"sourceAttributes":{"name":"Song.6.4-10","product":"TyndaleOpenStudyNotes","typename":"StudyNote"},"sourceBookAppCode":"SNG","sourceChapterCoverage":[6],"sourceItemFragmentSha256":"5a6aeebf4dfce492d05f4e531288716f57814638baabcea6db9940d4c31a7827","sourceItemName":"Song.6.4-10","sourceMember":"Tyndale Open Study Notes/StudyNotes.xml","sourceOrder":8169,"sourceReferenceException":false,"sourceReferenceMappingEvidence":{"appCode":"SNG","canonicalOSIS":"Song","method":"official_book_intro_ref_title","sourceOSIS":"Song"},"sourceReferenceStatus":"canonical","sourceRefsExact":"Song.6.4-10","sourceRelease":"1.25","sourceTitleExact":null,"supplementPointersNotIncludedAsContent":[],"title":"Song.6.4-10","type":"StudyNote"},{"chapterAssociationStatus":"canonical_reference","content":[{"c":[{"c":[{"href":"?bref=Song.6.4","r":"Song.6.4","rawR":"Song.6.4","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Song.6.4","value":"Song.6.4"}],"t":"ref","v":"6:4"}],"sourceClass":"sn-ref","t":"span"}," ",{"c":["Tirzah"],"sourceClass":"sn-excerpt","t":"span"}," was a city-state as early as Joshua’s time; it eventually became great enough to be the capital of the northern kingdom (",{"href":"?bref=Josh.12.24","r":"Josh.12.24","rawR":"Josh.12.24","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Josh.12.24","value":"Josh.12.24"}],"t":"ref","v":"Josh 12:24"},"; ",{"href":"?bref=1Kgs.15.33","r":"1Kgs.15.33","rawR":"1Kgs.15.33","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"1Kgs.15.33","value":"1Kgs.15.33"}],"t":"ref","v":"1 Kgs 15:33"},"). The town’s name means “pleasant.” • ",{"c":["Jerusalem"],"sourceClass":"sn-excerpt","t":"span"}," was the capital of David’s and Solomon’s united kingdom and after that, the most important city of Judah."],"sourceClass":"sn-text","t":"p"}],"id":"StudyNote:Song.6.4","refs":[{"book":"SNG","endChapter":6,"endVerse":4,"startChapter":6,"startVerse":4}],"sourceAttributes":{"name":"Song.6.4","product":"TyndaleOpenStudyNotes","typename":"StudyNote"},"sourceBookAppCode":"SNG","sourceChapterCoverage":[6],"sourceItemFragmentSha256":"e87af52f348190a9af68f855e61949d81316fa12087ff157dedb25197e68d793","sourceItemName":"Song.6.4","sourceMember":"Tyndale Open Study Notes/StudyNotes.xml","sourceOrder":8170,"sourceReferenceException":false,"sourceReferenceMappingEvidence":{"appCode":"SNG","canonicalOSIS":"Song","method":"official_book_intro_ref_title","sourceOSIS":"Song"},"sourceReferenceStatus":"canonical","sourceRefsExact":"Song.6.4","sourceRelease":"1.25","sourceTitleExact":null,"supplementPointersNotIncludedAsContent":[],"title":"Song.6.4","type":"StudyNote"},{"chapterAssociationStatus":"canonical_reference","content":[{"c":[{"c":[{"href":"?bref=Song.6.8-9","r":"Song.6.8-9","rawR":"Song.6.8-9","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Song.6.8-9","value":"Song.6.8-9"}],"t":"ref","v":"6:8-9"}],"sourceClass":"sn-ref","t":"span"}," If the Song were an allegory of the Lord’s love for his people, it would provide a very weak parallel, since Solomon’s excesses in marrying do a poor job of picturing God’s love and faithfulness (see ",{"href":"?bref=1Kgs.11.1-13","r":"1Kgs.11.1-13","rawR":"1Kgs.11.1-13","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"1Kgs.11.1-13","value":"1Kgs.11.1-13"}],"t":"ref","v":"1 Kgs 11:1-13"},"). • ",{"c":["sixty queens:"],"sourceClass":"sn-excerpt","t":"span"}," Many of Solomon’s wives were obtained by marrying into international treaties (see ",{"href":"?bref=1Kgs.11.1-3","r":"1Kgs.11.1-3","rawR":"1Kgs.11.1-3","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"1Kgs.11.1-3","value":"1Kgs.11.1-3"}],"t":"ref","v":"1 Kgs 11:1-3"},", ",{"href":"?bref=1Kgs.11.8","r":"1Kgs.11.8","rawR":"1Kgs.11.8","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"1Kgs.11.8","value":"1Kgs.11.8"}],"t":"ref","v":"8"},"). • ",{"c":["Concubines"],"sourceClass":"sn-excerpt","t":"span"}," were wives who had secondary status, lower than a wife but higher than a slave. Concubines were acquired by war, debt repayment, or purchase. • In the anthology view, the reference to ",{"c":["queens"],"sourceClass":"ital","t":"span"}," and ",{"c":["concubines"],"sourceClass":"ital","t":"span"}," simply means that the woman herself is more desirable than all other women."],"sourceClass":"sn-text","t":"p"}],"id":"StudyNote:Song.6.8-9","refs":[{"book":"SNG","endChapter":6,"endVerse":9,"startChapter":6,"startVerse":8}],"sourceAttributes":{"name":"Song.6.8-9","product":"TyndaleOpenStudyNotes","typename":"StudyNote"},"sourceBookAppCode":"SNG","sourceChapterCoverage":[6],"sourceItemFragmentSha256":"1adad0689815e6e3939009225081020a7ecb316aa7c3913f1301f50441e5c35a","sourceItemName":"Song.6.8-9","sourceMember":"Tyndale Open Study Notes/StudyNotes.xml","sourceOrder":8171,"sourceReferenceException":false,"sourceReferenceMappingEvidence":{"appCode":"SNG","canonicalOSIS":"Song","method":"official_book_intro_ref_title","sourceOSIS":"Song"},"sourceReferenceStatus":"canonical","sourceRefsExact":"Song.6.8-9","sourceRelease":"1.25","sourceTitleExact":null,"supplementPointersNotIncludedAsContent":[],"title":"Song.6.8-9","type":"StudyNote"},{"chapterAssociationStatus":"canonical_reference","content":[{"c":[{"c":[{"href":"?bref=Song.6.9","r":"Song.6.9","rawR":"Song.6.9","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Song.6.9","value":"Song.6.9"}],"t":"ref","v":"6:9"}],"sourceClass":"sn-ref","t":"span"}," ",{"c":["sing her praises:"],"sourceClass":"sn-excerpt","t":"span"}," Literally ",{"c":["called her blessed."],"sourceClass":"ital","t":"span"}," If the Song is a drama, the woman has left Solomon’s harem, and the singing took place in the past, when the woman was still there."],"sourceClass":"sn-text","t":"p"}],"id":"StudyNote:Song.6.9","refs":[{"book":"SNG","endChapter":6,"endVerse":9,"startChapter":6,"startVerse":9}],"sourceAttributes":{"name":"Song.6.9","product":"TyndaleOpenStudyNotes","typename":"StudyNote"},"sourceBookAppCode":"SNG","sourceChapterCoverage":[6],"sourceItemFragmentSha256":"6fe9d32fd4984f355315e5216b2b6a018cf55127485076d1b8b17d8606dd0261","sourceItemName":"Song.6.9","sourceMember":"Tyndale Open Study Notes/StudyNotes.xml","sourceOrder":8172,"sourceReferenceException":false,"sourceReferenceMappingEvidence":{"appCode":"SNG","canonicalOSIS":"Song","method":"official_book_intro_ref_title","sourceOSIS":"Song"},"sourceReferenceStatus":"canonical","sourceRefsExact":"Song.6.9","sourceRelease":"1.25","sourceTitleExact":null,"supplementPointersNotIncludedAsContent":[],"title":"Song.6.9","type":"StudyNote"},{"chapterAssociationStatus":"canonical_reference","content":[{"c":[{"c":[{"href":"?bref=Song.6.10","r":"Song.6.10","rawR":"Song.6.10","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Song.6.10","value":"Song.6.10"}],"t":"ref","v":"6:10"}],"sourceClass":"sn-ref","t":"span"}," The poem ends as it began in ",{"href":"?bref=Song.6.4","r":"Song.6.4","rawR":"Song.6.4","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Song.6.4","value":"Song.6.4"}],"t":"ref","v":"6:4"},": The woman’s majesty, like an ",{"c":["army with billowing banners"],"sourceClass":"sn-excerpt","t":"span"}," in full display, makes the knees knock and the heart flutter."],"sourceClass":"sn-text","t":"p"}],"id":"StudyNote:Song.6.10","refs":[{"book":"SNG","endChapter":6,"endVerse":10,"startChapter":6,"startVerse":10}],"sourceAttributes":{"name":"Song.6.10","product":"TyndaleOpenStudyNotes","typename":"StudyNote"},"sourceBookAppCode":"SNG","sourceChapterCoverage":[6],"sourceItemFragmentSha256":"c4173f2adcd9b033afc45f1dfaadbc33f43657d86defe6e5d962e39f1a075add","sourceItemName":"Song.6.10","sourceMember":"Tyndale Open Study Notes/StudyNotes.xml","sourceOrder":8173,"sourceReferenceException":false,"sourceReferenceMappingEvidence":{"appCode":"SNG","canonicalOSIS":"Song","method":"official_book_intro_ref_title","sourceOSIS":"Song"},"sourceReferenceStatus":"canonical","sourceRefsExact":"Song.6.10","sourceRelease":"1.25","sourceTitleExact":null,"supplementPointersNotIncludedAsContent":[],"title":"Song.6.10","type":"StudyNote"},{"chapterAssociationStatus":"canonical_reference","content":[{"c":[{"c":[{"href":"?bref=Song.6.11","r":"Song.6.11","rawR":"Song.6.11","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Song.6.11","value":"Song.6.11"}],"t":"ref","v":"6:11"}],"sourceClass":"sn-ref","t":"span"}," ",{"c":["I went down:"],"sourceClass":"sn-excerpt","t":"span"}," There is ambiguity over who is speaking here. The NLT presents it as the beginning of the young woman’s speech, though it could just as plausibly be a continuation of the young man’s. The trip to the ",{"c":["walnut"],"sourceClass":"sn-excerpt","t":"span"}," grove is a poetic way of expressing desire for a romantic meeting."],"sourceClass":"sn-text","t":"p"}],"id":"StudyNote:Song.6.11","refs":[{"book":"SNG","endChapter":6,"endVerse":11,"startChapter":6,"startVerse":11}],"sourceAttributes":{"name":"Song.6.11","product":"TyndaleOpenStudyNotes","typename":"StudyNote"},"sourceBookAppCode":"SNG","sourceChapterCoverage":[6],"sourceItemFragmentSha256":"eb4898385968ffd477a7949eabad48f607ae85e213fc70c5dec9c5974c793f1f","sourceItemName":"Song.6.11","sourceMember":"Tyndale Open Study Notes/StudyNotes.xml","sourceOrder":8174,"sourceReferenceException":false,"sourceReferenceMappingEvidence":{"appCode":"SNG","canonicalOSIS":"Song","method":"official_book_intro_ref_title","sourceOSIS":"Song"},"sourceReferenceStatus":"canonical","sourceRefsExact":"Song.6.11","sourceRelease":"1.25","sourceTitleExact":null,"supplementPointersNotIncludedAsContent":[],"title":"Song.6.11","type":"StudyNote"},{"chapterAssociationStatus":"canonical_reference","content":[{"c":[{"c":[{"href":"?bref=Song.6.12","r":"Song.6.12","rawR":"Song.6.12","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Song.6.12","value":"Song.6.12"}],"t":"ref","v":"6:12"}],"sourceClass":"sn-ref","t":"span"}," ",{"c":["to the chariot of a noble man:"],"sourceClass":"sn-excerpt","t":"span"}," Desire leads to the union of the man and the woman. There may be support here for Solomon being the husband, though this description could be a metaphor for the joys of romance (cp. ",{"href":"?bref=Song.1.16-17","r":"Song.1.16-17","rawR":"Song.1.16-17","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Song.1.16-17","value":"Song.1.16-17"}],"t":"ref","v":"1:16-17"},")."],"sourceClass":"sn-text","t":"p"}],"id":"StudyNote:Song.6.12","refs":[{"book":"SNG","endChapter":6,"endVerse":12,"startChapter":6,"startVerse":12}],"sourceAttributes":{"name":"Song.6.12","product":"TyndaleOpenStudyNotes","typename":"StudyNote"},"sourceBookAppCode":"SNG","sourceChapterCoverage":[6],"sourceItemFragmentSha256":"420fdcd5f107e1d73c7e3140704ab0eda770c6e8fff923b10316d07b53f0419f","sourceItemName":"Song.6.12","sourceMember":"Tyndale Open Study Notes/StudyNotes.xml","sourceOrder":8175,"sourceReferenceException":false,"sourceReferenceMappingEvidence":{"appCode":"SNG","canonicalOSIS":"Song","method":"official_book_intro_ref_title","sourceOSIS":"Song"},"sourceReferenceStatus":"canonical","sourceRefsExact":"Song.6.12","sourceRelease":"1.25","sourceTitleExact":null,"supplementPointersNotIncludedAsContent":[],"title":"Song.6.12","type":"StudyNote"},{"chapterAssociationStatus":"canonical_reference","content":[{"c":[{"c":[{"href":"?bref=Song.6.13-7.9","r":"Song.6.13-7.9","rawR":"Song.6.13-7.9","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Song.6.13-7.9","value":"Song.6.13-7.9"}],"t":"ref","v":"6:13–7:9"}],"sourceClass":"sn-ref","t":"span"}," An interaction between the young women of Jerusalem and the man introduces his last major description of the woman’s beauty and his desire to hold her (see ",{"href":"?item=Song.4.1-5.1_StudyNote_Filament","id":"Song.4.1-5.1_StudyNote_Filament","rawId":"Song.4.1-5.1_StudyNote_Filament","t":"link","v":"study note on 4:1–5:1"},")."],"sourceClass":"sn-text","t":"p"}],"id":"StudyNote:Song.6.13-7.9","refs":[{"book":"SNG","endChapter":7,"endVerse":9,"startChapter":6,"startVerse":13}],"sourceAttributes":{"name":"Song.6.13-7.9","product":"TyndaleOpenStudyNotes","typename":"StudyNote"},"sourceBookAppCode":"SNG","sourceChapterCoverage":[6,7],"sourceItemFragmentSha256":"b09afae5b2978df503f13f720eacae74ef25b4652f75c0b300622f65447e4d30","sourceItemName":"Song.6.13-7.9","sourceMember":"Tyndale Open Study Notes/StudyNotes.xml","sourceOrder":8176,"sourceReferenceException":false,"sourceReferenceMappingEvidence":{"appCode":"SNG","canonicalOSIS":"Song","method":"official_book_intro_ref_title","sourceOSIS":"Song"},"sourceReferenceStatus":"canonical","sourceRefsExact":"Song.6.13-7.9","sourceRelease":"1.25","sourceTitleExact":null,"supplementPointersNotIncludedAsContent":[],"title":"Song.6.13-7.9","type":"StudyNote"},{"chapterAssociationStatus":"canonical_reference","content":[{"c":[{"c":[{"href":"?bref=Song.6.13","r":"Song.6.13","rawR":"Song.6.13","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Song.6.13","value":"Song.6.13"}],"t":"ref","v":"6:13"}],"sourceClass":"sn-ref","t":"span"}," ",{"c":["Return:"],"sourceClass":"sn-excerpt","t":"span"}," Or ",{"c":["turn around."],"sourceClass":"ital","t":"span"}," • ",{"c":["Shulam:"],"sourceClass":"sn-excerpt","t":"span"}," The place is not known, though some have identified it with ",{"c":["Shunem"],"sourceClass":"ital","t":"span"}," (",{"href":"?bref=1Kgs.1.3","r":"1Kgs.1.3","rawR":"1Kgs.1.3","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"1Kgs.1.3","value":"1Kgs.1.3"}],"t":"ref","v":"1 Kgs 1:3"},"; ",{"href":"?bref=2Kgs.4.11-12","r":"2Kgs.4.11-12","rawR":"2Kgs.4.11-12","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"2Kgs.4.11-12","value":"2Kgs.4.11-12"}],"t":"ref","v":"2 Kgs 4:11-12"},"). ",{"c":["Maid of Shulam"],"sourceClass":"sn-excerpt","t":"span"}," (Hebrew ",{"c":["shulammith"],"sourceClass":"hebrew","t":"span"},") might be a wordplay on the name Solomon (Hebrew ",{"c":["shelomoh"],"sourceClass":"hebrew","t":"span"},{"c":[")"],"sourceClass":"ital","t":"span"},". It would then be equivalent to saying “Solomon’s maiden.” • ",{"c":["as she moves so gracefully between two lines of dancers?"],"sourceClass":"sn-excerpt","t":"span"}," The man praises the woman’s grace and beauty to everyone."],"sourceClass":"sn-text","t":"p"}],"id":"StudyNote:Song.6.13","refs":[{"book":"SNG","endChapter":6,"endVerse":13,"startChapter":6,"startVerse":13}],"sourceAttributes":{"name":"Song.6.13","product":"TyndaleOpenStudyNotes","typename":"StudyNote"},"sourceBookAppCode":"SNG","sourceChapterCoverage":[6],"sourceItemFragmentSha256":"62f5200222decdc3dbd71b367b53edf0551b12b800f9f2439fa4f5a2e422248e","sourceItemName":"Song.6.13","sourceMember":"Tyndale Open Study Notes/StudyNotes.xml","sourceOrder":8177,"sourceReferenceException":false,"sourceReferenceMappingEvidence":{"appCode":"SNG","canonicalOSIS":"Song","method":"official_book_intro_ref_title","sourceOSIS":"Song"},"sourceReferenceStatus":"canonical","sourceRefsExact":"Song.6.13","sourceRelease":"1.25","sourceTitleExact":null,"supplementPointersNotIncludedAsContent":[],"title":"Song.6.13","type":"StudyNote"}],"version":"v2-a101d843a2f0cd3fc0c6"}